===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|ah}}, {{m|enm|aa}}, {{m|enm|a|t=ah}}, of {{onomatopoeic|en|title=imitative origin}}, or from {{inh|en|ang|ēa}}, {{m|ang|*eah|t=oh, alas}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*a}}, {{m|gmw-pro|*ah|t=ah}}. Earliest recorded use is circa 1175 in the ''[[Ormulum]]'': ''A, Maȝȝstre! icc wat tatt tu full wiss Arrt Godess Sune'' ("Ah, Master! I know for sure that thou art God's Son"). Some propose that the Middle English is borrowed from {{der|en|fro|a|t=ah!, oh!, hey!}} (represented by modern {{cog|fr|ah}}).<ref>{{R:Lexico}}</ref><ref>{{R:Online_Etymology_Dictionary}}</ref>

Compare also {{cog|fy|a}}, {{m|fy|ah|t=ah}}, {{cog|nl|a}}, {{m|nl|ah|t=ah}}, {{cog|gml|a|t=ah}}, {{cog|goh|a}}, {{m|goh|aa}}, {{m|goh|ah|t=ah, oh}} (whence modern {{cog|de|ah}}), {{cog|fo|áh|t=oh, ah, alas}}, {{cog|is|æ}}, {{m|is|ai|t=ah, oh}}, {{cog|la|ah|t=ah}}.

